.SH "BUGS"
\fBkill \-9\fR (\fBSIGKILL\fR) is sent to every process in the tree without first attempting a graceful shutdown with \fBSIGTERM\fR\. This prevents the workflow from performing any cleanup (flushing buffers, releasing locks, writing final provenance records) before termination\.
.P
\fBexit \-1\fR is used for error exits\. POSIX does not guarantee the behaviour of negative exit codes; most shells interpret \fB\-1\fR as 255, but this is not portable\.
.P
The \fBLOGIN_PID=\fR assignment inside the running branch is empty and unused, suggesting incomplete logic that was never finished\.
.P
\fBpidtree\fR uses \fBdeclare \-A\fR associative arrays, requiring bash 4\.0 or later\. It will fail silently on bash 3\.x (the default on macOS)\.
.P
The \fB/proc/<pid>\fR check used to determine whether the process is running is Linux\-specific and will not work on macOS or other non\-Linux systems\.
.P
\fB$RECORD_PID_FILE\fR and \fB$RECORD_SLURM_PREFIX\fR are unquoted in several places; values containing spaces or shell metacharacters may cause unexpected behaviour\.
.P
The Slurm \fBgrep\fR pattern \fB$RECORD_SLURM_PREFIX'\.*'\fR concatenates a shell variable with a literal \fB\.*\fR, which is a basic regular expression passed to \fBgrep\fR\. If \fBRECORD_SLURM_PREFIX\fR is unset, all jobs in the queue will be matched and cancelled\.
.P
If any \fBkill \-9\fR call fails (e\.g\. due to a permissions error or a PID that has already exited), the error is silently ignored and the loop continues\. The PID file is still removed regardless of whether all kills succeeded\.
